      "facts_pool": "Facts Pool",
      "famous_clip_showing_real_octopus_camou": "Famous clip showing real octopus camouflage in real time on coral reef. Slow-motion footage of color change + texture matching.",
      "famous_for_carrying_coconut_halves_acr": "Famous for carrying coconut halves across the sea floor — the only documented invertebrate tool use. Walks BIPEDALLY on two back arms while carrying coconuts. Picking up a shelter costs LESS stamina (innate tool-use bonus). Carries shelters faster. Indo-Pacific sand-flat habitat — best on sand substrate.",
      "famous_for_carrying_coconut_halves_acr_2": "Famous for carrying coconut halves across the sea floor. The first documented case of tool use in an invertebrate involving DELAYED object manipulation. Walks BIPEDALLY on its two back arms while carrying coconuts (Huffard et al. 2005, Science).",
      "famous_for_mimicry_not_for_tool_use": "Famous for mimicry, not for tool use.",
      "famous_for_synchronized_bioluminescent": "Famous for synchronized bioluminescent mating displays. Tens of millions individuals at Toyama Bay spawning each May.",
      "famous_individuals": "Famous Individuals",
      "fast_responses_mixed_outcomes": "Fast responses, mixed outcomes",
      "female_cephalopods_often_store_sperm_f": "Female cephalopods often store sperm from multiple matings + selectively use it later — a phenomenon called CRYPTIC FEMALE CHOICE. Documented in Octopus mimus + others.",
      "female_cuttlefish_have_been_observed_s": "Female cuttlefish have been observed selecting LARGER spermatophores over smaller ones — likely a form of cryptic mate choice.",
      "female_finds_a_secure_den_attaches_egg": "Female finds a secure den, attaches eggs, then stays with them continuously — no hunting, no eating. She cleans the eggs of fungal + bacterial growth + ventilates them with siphon water. The optic gland releases steroid + neuropeptide hormones that drive the brooding behavior AND her senescence.",
      "female_graneledone_boreopacifica_deep_": "Female Graneledone boreopacifica (deep-sea octopus) brood their eggs for 53 MONTHS — the longest known parental investment of any animal.",
      "female_octopuses_attach_50_000_500_000": "Female octopuses attach 50,000-500,000 eggs to a den ceiling. Each egg is a translucent capsule on a stalk. The female guards them — continuously cleaning, oxygenating with siphon-pulse currents, and defending from would-be predators — for the entire incubation. She does not eat during this time. In Graneledone boreopacifica (deep-sea), the brooding period of 53 months is the longest known parental investment of ANY animal.",
      "female_octopuses_brood_eggs_and_then_d": "Female octopuses brood eggs and then die. What hormone system drives this?",
      "female_octopuses_brood_eggs_for_months": "Female octopuses brood eggs for months without eating, then die. What evolutionary trade-off does this represent? Why might this strategy have evolved?",
      "female_octopuses_brood_their_eggs_for_": "Female octopuses brood their eggs for weeks or months without eating, then die soon after the eggs hatch. This isn't just exhaustion — it's a genetically programmed senescence driven by a single endocrine organ: the OPTIC GLAND.\n\nThe optic gland is a small organ (typically <1 mm in diameter) located behind each eye in cephalopods. After mating, the gland begins releasing a cascade of hormones — primarily steroids (including 7-dehydrocholesterol, 7β-OH-cholesterol, and estradiol) plus neuropeptides. These hormones together: (1) drive the female to find a den + lay eggs; (2) cause her to stop eating; (3) accelerate aging in multiple organ systems.\n\nThe foundational experiment is Wodinsky 1977. He surgically REMOVED the optic glands from brooding female octopuses. The treated females ABANDONED their eggs, RESUMED eating, and LIVED MUCH LONGER than control females. This established that the optic gland was the master controller of brooding + senescence — not the act of egg-laying itself.\n\nThe Wang & Ragsdale lab has spent the past decade resolving the molecular cascade. They've identified at least 4-7 distinct hormones produced by the optic gland, each contributing different parts of the brooding + senescence program. The 2022 paper (Wang et al., Curr. Biol.) found multiple steroid signals (β-sitosterol, 7β-OH-cholesterol, estradiol, etc.) that drive the cascade. This is more complex than the original picture suggested.\n\nWhy might this exist? Evolutionarily, octopuses are SEMELPAROUS — they reproduce once + die. The optic gland mechanism is one of the cleanest molecular models of programmed senescence in any animal. Understanding it has implications for aging research broadly. If we can identify the conserved pathways, we might learn something about vertebrate senescence too.\n\nClinical applications? Not directly — humans aren't semelparous. But the principle (a single endocrine signal driving a coordinated multi-organ aging program) is informative for understanding how aging itself works mechanistically.\n\n(Sources: Wodinsky, 1977; Wang & Ragsdale, 2018; Wang et al., 2022 — Curr. Biol.)",
      "female_octopuses_guard_their_eggs_for_": "Female octopuses guard their eggs for 1-10 months without eating, dying shortly after the hatch. This terminal investment is called semelparity.",
